chimpanzees have a lot on their minds  researchers have been trying to get  inside their heads for decades and along  the way they've discovered new twists in  how chimps think and behave 50 years ago  Jane Goodall chimps tick blades of grass  and twigs into soft termite mounds to  collect and slurp up the nutritious bugs  deep in the forests of the Republic of  the Congo chimps must deal with huge  termite mounds covered in hard crust  these Apes wrap one or both hands around  sturdy twigs to bust through the crust  then they use sutler grips on smaller  twigs to clean termite tunnels to snag a  snack chimps don't roast the termites  before eating them but they may  understand that food can be cooked and  maybe even prefer some foods roasted  when given the option animals at an  African chimp sanctuary usually chose  too much cooked potato slices over our  they were also willing to wait a full  minute for three cooked potato slices  rather than downing a raw slice right  away as evidence of chimps complex  behavior mounts so does controversy over  whether the Apes can reveal anything  about ancient members of the human  evolutionary family four million years  ago ancient hominids may have forged for  termites and savored foods naturally  roasted in wild fires much as chimps do  today that's one theory but some  anthropologists aren't convinced that  debate will probably continue and so  will the search to uncover exactly  what's going on in a chimps head
 

